# Issue Workflow

Work flows through Linear. One issue at a time, planned before it is implemented.

The planner follows the `plan-ticket` skill. The implementing agent follows
`AGENTS.md`.

## Stages

1. An issue is picked from `Dashi F&F Launch`, respecting milestone order and
   `blocked by` relations.
2. A plan is written and approved. After approval:
   - **Figma** (read or write: design, edits, or pixel-matching a design in
     code): post the plan as a Linear comment **without** `@Cursor`. The human
     runs a local `agent` session. Cloud Agents cannot use Figma MCP.
   - **Otherwise**: post a comment that begins with `@Cursor`, which starts the
     cloud implementor.
   The ticket stays at `Ready`.
3. The implementing agent builds it and marks the PR ready. Then the human
   reviews.
4. Merge once checks are green, Bugbot has had its pass, and the human has
   approved.

## Figma

Cloud Agents cannot use Figma MCP. Brainstorm, edits, reads, and any
implementation that must match Figma run in a **local** session with Figma MCP.

The planner reads (and, on design tickets, writes) Figma while planning. The
plan may include file URLs, node IDs, and screenshots so a human can review it.
Screenshots are not the spec: the implementor uses `get_design_context` on the
live file.

Do not `@Cursor` those tickets. GitHub review-followup resumes a cloud agent,
which also has no Figma; continue visual follow-up in a local session.

## Status

- `Ready` means the ticket has an approved plan. On a non-Figma ticket,
  `@Cursor` on that comment starts the cloud implementor and does not change
  the status. On a Figma ticket, `Ready` means the local session can start.
- `In Progress` means a draft PR exists. The GitHub integration sets this when
  the agent opens the PR on init.
- `In Review` means the PR left draft. The GitHub integration sets this when
  `gh pr ready` takes.
- Never set Linear status by hand. The GitHub integration moves the ticket.

## Feedback

- **Code-level feedback**: inline on the GitHub PR diff, then **submit it as a
  review**. Submitting is what matters: `review-followup.yml` fires on the
  submitted review and hands it to the agent that opened the PR. Comments left
  without submitting a review reach nobody. A review sweeps up every thread
  still open, including ones from earlier rounds.
- **One agent owns a task end to end.** It keeps the conversation and workspace
  from its first run, so it still holds the plan.
- **Fixes go through a GitHub review.** A later `@Cursor` on Linear starts a
  new agent from `main`, which cannot see the branch under review. Linear's
  Automations behave the same way.
- **Plan deviations ship in the PR.** The implementing agent lists them under
  **Plan deviations** in the handoff comment and leaves the Linear plan as the
  human wrote it.

## Bugbot

- Bugbot **cannot see project rules or `AGENTS.md`.** Its guidance lives in
  `.cursor/BUGBOT.md`.
- Autofix is **off**. Bugbot comments, the implementing agent fixes.
- It runs **once**, automatically, when the PR leaves draft. Draft reviews are
  off so the first-push stub is skipped.
- It is triage, not a merge gate. Do not require the `Cursor Bugbot` check in
  branch protection as a findings gate; the check is `neutral` when it reports
  issues.
- `review-followup.yml` only fires on a submitted review, so if Bugbot posts
  standalone comments a human has to relay them.

## Tooling

`scripts/review_followup.ts` is run by `.github/workflows/review-followup.yml`.
It talks to the Cursor Cloud Agents **v1** REST API over plain `fetch`. The
workflow needs `CURSOR_API_KEY` as a repository secret. The repo has no
dependencies.

## Shared conventions

- **Quarantine with an owner.** A known failure stays greppable: suppress it as
  narrowly as possible and name the issue that fixes it.
- **One owner per directory at a time.** Two in-flight issues touching the same
  directory will conflict.
- **One way to do a thing.** No alias, twin API, or overlapping concept for a
  job that already has a way. Planning, implementation, and review each catch
  this; see those stages.
